.Dd September 27, 2026
.Dt ACCEPT 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm accept ,
.Nm accept4
.Nd accept a queued connection
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In sys/socket.h
.Ft int
.Fn accept "int fd" "struct sockaddr *restrict addr" "socklen_t *restrict addrlen"
.Ft int
.Fn accept4 "int fd" "struct sockaddr *restrict addr" "socklen_t *restrict addrlen" "int flags"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn accept
removes a completed connection from the accept queue of
.Fa fd
and returns a new descriptor for it.
.Fa fd
must refer to a listening TCP socket, or a listening
.Dv AF_UNIX
stream or sequenced-packet socket, which
.Xr unix 7
describes.
The new descriptor is blocking and has close-on-exec clear, whatever the
listener's flags.
.Pp
.Fn accept4
is
.Fn accept
with
.Fa flags
for the new descriptor, as Linux takes them:
.Dv SOCK_CLOEXEC
sets its close-on-exec flag, and
.Dv SOCK_NONBLOCK
sets
.Dv O_NONBLOCK
on its open-file description.
Any other bit in
.Fa flags
gives
.Er EINVAL
before the descriptor is looked at.
.Fn accept
is
.Fn accept4
with
.Fa flags
0.
.Pp
If the queue is empty, the call waits for a connection.
On a listener with
.Dv O_NONBLOCK
set, an empty queue instead gives
.Er EAGAIN .
An unblocked, nonignored pending signal interrupts the wait with
.Er EINTR .
.Pp
If
.Fa addr
is non-null, the call reports the peer's address.
Before the call,
.Pf * Fa addrlen
is the capacity of
.Fa addr
in bytes.
At most that many bytes of the address are copied, and
.Pf * Fa addrlen
is set to its whole length even if the result was truncated.
A TCP peer is reported as its IPv4 address and port in a 16-byte
.Vt sockaddr_in ,
whose family is in host byte order and whose address and port are in
network byte order.
An
.Dv AF_UNIX
peer is reported by the name it was bound to, as
.Xr unix 7
describes; a client with no name, which is the usual case, is reported
as the family alone, a length of 2.
If
.Fa addr
is null, no address is returned and
.Fa addrlen
is not used; if
.Fa addr
is non-null and
.Fa addrlen
is null, the call fails with
.Er EFAULT ,
as in Linux.
The new descriptor is reserved before the connection leaves the queue
and refers to it only once the address has been copied, so another
thread sharing the descriptor table cannot use or close it before
.Fn accept
returns.
An address-copy failure frees the descriptor and discards the dequeued
connection.
.Pp
A TCP listener's accept queue holds at most 15 completed connections,
whatever the backlog
.Xr listen 2
was given.
The TCP listener drops an incoming SYN when the free-frame count is at
or below the memory reserve, before allocating the connection.
Such a drop produces no error from
.Fn accept .
An
.Dv AF_UNIX
listener queues connections up to its backlog, as
.Xr unix 7
describes.
.Pp
The kernel interface is
.Xr socketcall 2
operation 5 for
.Fn accept
and operation 18 for
.Fn accept4 ,
whose fourth argument word is
.Fa flags ,
or the direct call
.Fn accept4 ,
number 364.
Musl's
.Fn accept4
sets the flags with
.Xr fcntl 2
itself when the kernel refuses them.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
On success,
.Fn accept
and
.Fn accept4
return the new descriptor.
On failure it returns -1 and sets
.Va errno .
The raw kernel call returns a negative error number on failure.
.Sh EXAMPLES
The default-suite probe
.Pa tests/loopprobe.c
binds a TCP listener to port 7200, connects a second socket to
.Ql 127.0.0.1:7200 ,
accepts with both address pointers null, and exchanges data through the
accepted connection.
.Pa tests/musl/sockflagmusl.c
calls operation 18 by number on a listener made with
.Dv SOCK_CLOEXEC
and
.Dv SOCK_NONBLOCK :
it checks that an empty queue gives
.Er EAGAIN ,
that an unknown flag gives
.Er EINVAL ,
and that connections from
.Ql 127.0.0.1
accepted with both flags and with none have exactly those flags.
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width EOPNOTSUPP
.It Er EAGAIN
The listener is nonblocking and its accept queue is empty.
.It Er EFAULT
The socketcall argument array cannot be read, the peer address or
length cannot be read or written as required, or
.Fa addr
is non-null and
.Fa addrlen
is null.
.It Er EINTR
A signal interrupted the wait.
.It Er EINVAL
The socket is a TCP datagram socket, or a stream or sequenced-packet
socket that is not listening; an
.Dv AF_UNIX
listener shut down for reading has no connection queued; or
.Fa flags
has a bit other than
.Dv SOCK_CLOEXEC
and
.Dv SOCK_NONBLOCK .
.It Er EMFILE
No descriptor or open-file description could be allocated for the
connection, which stays queued.
.It Er ENOTSOCK
.Fa fd
is invalid or does not refer to a socket.
.It Er EOPNOTSUPP
.Fa fd
refers to a raw socket or an
.Dv AF_UNIX
datagram socket.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr bind 2 ,
.Xr connect 2 ,
.Xr fcntl 2 ,
.Xr listen 2 ,
.Xr socket 2 ,
.Xr socketcall 2 ,
.Xr memory 7 ,
.Xr unix 7
